# Chats — cards, artifacts and shells

**Cards (agent-drawn payloads):**

- Each card kind has a twin parser (`*-payload.ts`) of the daemon's schema.
- Agent buttons (`action-chip.tsx`, `action-card.tsx`, `use-chat-actions.ts`): what is pressable is run state's `actions`, never a fold of the window. A press refused `RUN_BUSY` is retried by the hook (same seq, same `clientMessageId`) once the turn settles, never through the composer queue, which would send the text without recording the press. The shelf group sits after the stage chip, ahead of the running team chip. A team run presses through the team routes (the hook takes whichever API the open run needs; the chat route refuses a team run), and its busy refusals are asked again on `ACTION_RETRY_MS`, since its run state opens no turn. A button whose `publisher.agentLabel` is set (another team agent's) says so: `· <agent>` on the chip, `from <agent>` on the card, linking to its call's card, which it opens (`ChatActions.openCall`: the `call:<blockId>` thread flag, then a reveal, paging first when the card is off the window; the working-agents card's Show in chat does the same). A stop button lists what it stops (`spec.targets`: command and pid, plus what these start by the press) on its card and in the chip's tooltip before it is pressed, and its status reads `stopped` or `already stopped` (`alreadyStopped`).
- A command action opens `CommandConfirmDialog` from the chip, the card and the overflow row alike (the confirm state lives in `useChatActions`: `press` only sets `confirming`; one dialog per surface in `ChatProviders`), showing the exact argv, one quoted token per argument, and the folder. Nothing is sent until "Run command". The card shows the command (`Runs:`) before and after the press and, once it ends, its last run's status (exit code or error) even while the button stays live — a failed or reusable command stays pressable — plus a folded `Output` with the tail (`outputTail`, drawn through `AnsiText`, fold kept per thread). Only a message press starts a turn (`pressStartsTurn`), so only it raises a surface's working flag (the builder dock's).
- A recommendations card's knowledge items (`K-`) sit in a group of their own. A move's two sides are ticked and unticked together and each names the other (`movePartnerOf`), since the daemon refuses an Apply of one (`MOVE_SPLIT`).
- A system analysis's card (`sourceKind: 'system'`) draws `RecommendationsCard`'s `actions` in place of Apply: a split button — Create cards (default), Implement right away, Keep as report — whose first two open a project dialog with nothing preselected and Keep as report posts no project (`POST /v1/system-analyses/:runId/apply` through `SystemRecommendationsApplyContext`, provided by `Chats` with its project refs). Its `geniro` items (`G-`) are a group of their own, listed first; each item's badges read its outcome row's note (`filed <KEY>`, `filed to start <KEY>`, `started <KEY>`, failed). The parser (`recommendations-payload.ts`) is the twin of the daemon's card schema: `geniro`, `sourceKind: 'system'` and `addressesFlags`.
- A team card states the population beside the sample (`data-slot="analysis-population"`: the runs read of how many the team has, with the average over all of them) and in the projection's footnote, drawn from `teamStats.population`, which the daemon measures over every ledger row; never folded here. An external item (`X-`) sits in a group of its own, "Other repositories": applying it files a board card, so its outcome note names that card.
- `isCardEntry` (`transcript-groups.ts`) is the one predicate over card kinds. Never write a `'task-list' || …` chain.
- A new card tool also goes in `GENIRO_TOOL_NAMES`, which the compiler cannot check.
- Charts key rows by position (`s0`, `s1`, …), never by series name. `null` stays a gap.
- Metrics colour a delta by stated sentiment, never by sign.
- The gallery loads through `LocalImageLoaderContext`. It does that sequentially and only while open, releases images on fold, and uses no lightbox of its own. A picture named by URL arrives already downloaded by the daemon (a local path, with the URL as `source`); the renderer never fetches one. Each card shows its caption and description under the picture in one horizontally scrolling strip, and the viewer shows the description too.
- An A2UI card (`show_ui`: `a2ui-surface.tsx`, `a2ui-components.tsx` over the pure `a2ui-model.ts`, `use-surface.ts`): drawn at its `create` row only (`SurfaceEntry`), read whole from `GET …/surfaces/:surfaceId` and read again when run state's `surfaces` names a newer version, so later rows draw nothing of their own. Every catalog component is drawn with this design system's primitives, never `@a2ui/react`; Text is the chat's markdown with links, pictures and HTML stripped (`cardMarkdown`), and a link is an `openUrl` button through `ChatLink`. Values typed are saved as drafts (`useDebouncedPersist`), an obscured field's never; a draft refused `UI_SURFACE_CHANGED` is read again and sent again without the pointers the agent wrote, and a field the agent overwrote is marked (`overriddenDrafts`). A press resolves the button's `userMessage` and `context` here (a List template's scope is known only here), keeps one `clientMessageId` through its `RUN_BUSY` retries (on the chat's turn closing, on `ACTION_RETRY_MS` in a team run) and goes through the team route in a team run. Media arrives as base64 from the chat route: a picture as a `data:` URL, video and sound as a `blob:` URL made on Play and revoked on unmount (the renderer CSP's `media-src 'self' blob:`).
- Dynamic workflow: the roster is taken whole. A launch is recognised by a `workflow_info` row, never by tool name.
- `save_project_instructions` renders as an ordinary approval card (`diff-view.tsx` `editDiffOf`), drawing the daemon's unified hunks and the `P-` ids it carries out (`projectInstructionsCardOf`, `UnifiedDiff`) while its hazard scan reads the whole old and new text; the verdict row's `outcome` (`not-saved` draws "✗ Not saved") and `answer` say what Apply did. The card marks the words that changed inside paired lines (`UnifiedDiff` `wordLevel`). It is `'patch'` in `GENIRO_TOOLS` (`isProposedChange`); a refused `save_project_instructions` call keeps its row (`transcript-groups.ts`).
- A plan or question row that cannot be parsed falls back to the permission body.
- `propose_team` draws `TeamProposalCard` from its `approval_request` row (`team-proposal-payload.ts`, twin of the daemon's `TeamProposalSchema` / `TeamDiff` / `TeamProposalOutcome`); an unreadable payload falls back to the permission body. The agent's `summary` and `reason` (a change sent as edits, from any chat, always carries both) are drawn as its own words under the title. Saved / not saved is the verdict row's `outcome`, never the agent-bound message. A card whose team half changes nothing (`diff.identical`) says so in its badge and its saved line, and offers Open project instead of Open team where the surface can open one (`onOpenProject`; the project's own chat, already on that page, cannot). Its Approve gate scans every string the card can draw, raw (`proposalScanText`), the diff's old side included. `components/stage-graph-flow.tsx`, `lazy()` behind `StageGraph`, is the only chat-reachable importer of `@xyflow/react` and `elkjs`. The card draws the members (each with its public role and the blocks addressed to it, never its private instructions — those appear only in the change list, which quotes every field a change touches), the trigger member and the stages, and — as sections of their own beside the shared team, each a `UnifiedDiff` — a project's overlay and a project's instructions the same Approve writes (`overlay`, `project`, each read tolerantly and dropped whole when malformed), its approval line naming every file it changes; a proposal recorded in the node-graph shape is read through `teamFromLegacyGraph`. The change list comes first and is open; the members and the stage texts start folded, a fold still showing the members and stages a replacement adds or changes (`previewMarks`, `stageMarksOf`) and saying how many more it holds; every change in the list says what it is: an added member, block or stage lists each field it sets, read whole from the proposed team (`entryFields`), a removed one each field it held and a changed team field its old and new value, both quoted by the daemon (a card raised before those were quoted names them alone); and a long value is drawn as its sides, one under the other, a changed one quoted from where the two differ (`quoteTextChange`). The card stands across turns, so it counts as needs-input only while no turn streams (`standsAcrossTurns`, the daemon's `awaitingOf`).
- A command center thread's open request, relayed into the manager chat (`thread_question`, twin of the daemon's `ThreadQuestionCardInput`), draws `ThreadQuestionCard` (`thread-question-card.tsx`) around the thread's own `ApprovalCard`, never a second card body; an unreadable relay falls through to the permission body. Its verdict goes to the manager's own card id, which the daemon forwards to the thread, and a picture sent with the answer — pasted into the card, or staged in the manager composer (its paperclip) when the answer is given — goes to the thread's own message route, the answer text counting both (`threadQuestionRelayOfRow` in `Chats.tsx`'s `respondApproval`, `deliverRelayedImages`), never into a manager turn: refused RUN_BUSY by the turn the answer started, it is sent again on the thread's next settle, at most `RELAYED_IMAGES_SEND_ATTEMPTS` times. Once answered or closed its header says the thread asked, no longer that it waits; closed with no answer, it says so in place of the card. Above its thread head it offers Open chat (the `ThreadOpenContext` open, the command center's `showThread`) and Show a few messages, which unfolds in the card the thread's last `QUESTION_CONTEXT_MESSAGES` messages before its request (a call's question: the call's brief beside them) and the pages and pictures its asking turn published (`QUESTION_CONTEXT_VISUALS`, drawn by `question-context-visual.tsx` as the thread's own cards: `ThreadContextReaderContext`, provided by `Chats` and the command center through `threadContextReaderOf(chats, handle)` over `listRunItems` with `conversation=root`, a call's from its asking member's rows after the call's brief, since a member's pages stand in the run's own flow; `messagesBefore` cuts the page at the relay's `threadRequestId`, the page's newest messages when the request is not on it; the page URL, the pictures and what the user picks on a page (`answers`) are the thread's own run's, and no Share is offered there). Each is drawn only where its context is provided. A question row written with `attachments` (twin of the daemon's `utils/question-attachments.ts`, read by `question-attachments-payload.ts`, relayed verbatim on `ThreadQuestionCardInput`) draws ONLY those, through `question-attachments.tsx` — the page's key read from the thread run's own artifact listing at draw time, never stored; a page missing from it, or a listing that fails, draws the unavailable note; "+N more" from `moreAttachments`; chips on the folded card and on the thread's own `QuestionCard`, loading nothing until pressed — and the asking-turn derivation above is the LEGACY reader, used only for a row with no `attachments` key.
- In the command center's manager chat (`HostedThread.commandCenterManager`), a thread's Done card is drawn from the newest row announcing it only (`newestDoneSeqByRun`, `ThreadDoneNewestContext`), shows the board's live report when it has one (`CommandCenterCard.report`, the daemon's reading, never `RunDto.lastMessage`, which may be the user's; `threadReportsOf` → `HostedThread.threadReports` → `ThreadReportsContext`, the row's frozen summary otherwise), heads it with the thread's `ThreadHead` from `ThreadIdentityContext` — face, title, its pull request as a `ChatLink` to the address the board knows (`ThreadPullRequest.url`, a plain label where none is known), status, agent or team, board card and project — and its Dismiss folds it to one line per device (`useThreadFlag`). A `waitingItems` row (twin of the daemon's `WaitingItemsRow`) draws `WaitingItemsCard`: each item's live state from `useWaitingItems` (pending memory entries, waiting lessons, pending knowledge revisions, re-read on their change announces), drawn with the Settings rows themselves (`MemoryRow`, `LessonProposalRow`, `KnowledgeReview` in a dialog) and one quiet resolved line once it no longer waits; outside the manager chat its titles alone. The rest of what waits in Settings is no row at all: `SettingsAttentionCards` (`chats/settings-attention-cards.tsx`), drawn at the transcript's tail of the manager chat only, from the readings Settings itself draws — agent updates and signed-out accounts (`useCliAttention`'s detections, `useCliUpdates`, `useCliLogin` with `CliLoginProgress`), the Geniro update (`useUpdateState`; no buttons on a paired device, install and relaunch being `denyRemotely`), shares waiting on a sign-in one card per destination (`SignInAction`, never read on a paired device, the share routes being desktop only) and the undecided memory findings as one count whose Review conflicts reaches `App` through `HostedThread.onOpenMemoryConflicts` (Settings → Memory → Clean up on Conflicts) — so a card goes as soon as its reason does, wherever it was settled; the daemon lists are `useWaitingSet` reads, held only while the chat is `active`, so coming back to it reads them again. Each card's Dismiss folds it to one line on this device through the thread UI memory (`useCardDismissal` over `useThreadFlags`, one flag per item the card shows — a CLI's offered version, a signed-out account, the Geniro version, a waiting share, a memory finding), and it opens again only when an item it has not folded appears. Both kinds of card frame through `chats/notice-card.tsx`.
- Every agent-interaction event is ONE card, `AgentEventCard` (`agent-event-card.tsx`: the event line with its actions at its end, then the compact one-line `ThreadHead`, text rail folded at 8 lines, inert options, outcome line; reads no context; a thread's Done card wears the graph node's full head instead — `fullHead`: the title on two lines, the labels on their own — and keeps its time and actions in the node's foot, `ThreadFoot`), one `KIND_META` for every kind. Its wrappers: `ThreadEventCard` (another thread), `MemberEventCard` (a member of the run: its `MemberAvatar` face and CLI label, Open call through `RevealCallContext`, plain where no card is loaded) and `MonitorEventCard` (folded to ONE line through `AgentEventCard`'s `line` — the kind's icon, the monitor's title, its state chip (`monitorStatusOf`), its id, its agent and how much it printed, the kind and command in the line's tooltip — which opens, kept per thread, onto its command, timeout and whole output; nothing of the output shows while folded; a row without command or timeout opens onto its output alone). `transcript-item.tsx` routes to them: a `system` row `readAgentCallEvents` reads (`agent-event-payload.ts`: `wake`, `toldWorking`, `orphanQuestion`, else the stored sentences of rows written before those keys — anchored templates; an unrecognised info row keeps its grey note), a monitor notice, `call_question`, `call_answer` and a member's failed / cancelled / skipped `status`. A question's answer row is drawn on its card (`pairCallAnswers`, `CallAnswerPairsContext`, provided by `Chats` over `transcriptRows`) and not again on its own; so is the `system` row saying no turn was left to answer it, whose went-on / stopped words replace the answer row's (one card per unanswered question). Either row draws its own card only while its question is not loaded. A question its caller passed on to the user as its own question card while it was open (`passedOn`) draws no text or options, only who asked and that it was passed on. The user's bubbles and the run-level turn footer stay as they are. `thread-question-card.tsx` opens with the same `EventLine`.
- The plan and team cards share their note field and its send rule (`chats/verdict-note.tsx`); the hazard gate is `chats/text-hazard-gate.tsx`, shared with the permission card.
- A question card is drawn from the row's `questions`, never from a CLI's tool name. `secret` masks the field.
- `useOneShotVerdict` is the one verdict-timing contract.
- Only the latest task-list card shows its rows. Fold state derived from data takes a press override (`useThreadOverride`). Never seed derived open state into `useState`.
- A settled question card starts folded to its header, its first question and the answer. A relayed question folds with its `ThreadQuestionCard` as one card (`QuestionFold`, owned by `RelayedRequestCard` under the question card's own key): folded, only the question card shows, its header naming the thread; a relayed permission never folds, and one closed with no answer starts open, as an expired question card does.
- Approval cards scan for text hazards (`chats/text-hazards.ts`):
  - Bidi or invisible characters gate Approve until acknowledged.
  - The tick resets when the content changes. Deny is never gated.
  - The banner names codepoints and never prints the character.
  - Join scanned parts with spaces, never newlines. Ordinary emoji are exempt.

A `save_plugin` card (kind `plugin`, `SAVE_PLUGIN` in `geniro-tool.ts`) draws `PluginProposalCard` (`chats/plugin-proposal-card.tsx`): what it runs, where it shows, each button's asks-first, a preview from the sample, the change for an update. `pluginCardOf` is the twin of the daemon's `pluginCardInput`.

`VerdictFooter` (`chats/verdict-footer.tsx`) is the one Approve/Deny footer of the approval card and the plugin card: the hazard gate and the verdict line live there.

**Artifacts** (`published-artifact.ts`, `artifact-frame.tsx`):

- The frame's sandbox is the root `AGENTS.md`'s (_Constraints_, the artifact page's outbound call). The page is served by the daemon, never put in `srcdoc`, and loaded with `referrerPolicy="no-referrer"`.
- Into the frame go theme tokens and, once the page announces itself, the answers kept for it (`ArtifactAnswersContext`, provided by `Chats` with `ArtifactUrlContext`); out of it come its height, a link, a click on one of its own `data:` pictures — the picture or a link to it (shown in `ImageViewer`), an Escape the page did not act on (`closeTopEscapeLayer`, since a key pressed in the frame never reaches the renderer's document), a folder button's path (`window.geniro.showInFinder` through `useShowInFinder`, desktop only, only on a fresh click — `navigator.userActivation` — one per `OPEN_PATH_GAP_MS`, its failure shown under the frame in the frame's own words) and each answer the page changes (`answer`, with the page's save count, sent as a batch of changes after `ANSWERS_SAVE_DELAY_MS` and on unmount, sent again after `ANSWERS_RETRY_MS` when refused; the merged set comes back to the saving frame with the counts it confirmed, and `ArtifactAnswersStore` hands it to every other frame of the page). All by `postMessage`, filtered on `event.source`; the picture, the Escape, the folder and the answers only while the frame holds focus (`document.activeElement`), so a page cannot act on the app unprompted. Also out of it, focused or not: the reader's scroll gestures (`gesture`: a touch starting and ending, a wheel turned up), which never reach the renderer's document from inside the frame; the frame re-announces each as `FRAME_GESTURE_EVENT` bubbling from its `<iframe>`, and the transcript feeds it to its touch guard and upward intent (`scroll-follow.ts`), or a swipe over the page while the chat follows its tail reads as the layout moving it and is snapped back.
- Key the frame by `${artifactId}:${version}`.
- A `show_artifact` row is never claimed into a call card (`UNCLAIMABLE_KINDS`): a callee's page is drawn in the main flow, and the newest-card sweep spans the whole run.
- The panel list comes from `GET /v1/chats/:runId/artifacts` and answers empty on a run mismatch.
- A saved file bakes in the theme and restates the CSP as `ARTIFACT_FILE_CSP`, a twin of the daemon's `artifact-page.ts`. Insert them into a parsed `<head>` (`DOMParser`), never by text splice.
- A saved file must look like the framed page. The daemon's raw reading (`renderArtifactDocument`) carries the frame's base style (font, padding, box-sizing, centring) but not its transparent ground, and the export supplies the ground.
- Links are opened by the host, only for `http:`, `https:` and `mailto:` (`shared/link-schemes.ts`); an inline page's go to the chat browser by the chat-link rule, the full-screen dialog's always to the system browser (its page hides the pane). Main's `guardArtifactFrameNavigations` cancels any frame navigation away from the artifact route.
- Share (`artifact-share.tsx` over `use-artifact-sharing.ts`, provided as `ArtifactShareContext` by `Chats`): beside Save on the card and in the dialog header; null on a paired phone, which draws nothing. A press sends the current `themeVars()`. The link (key in its `#fragment`) is read from the daemon when the panel opens, never kept in the list — and only for a share that was uploaded (`sharedVersion > 0`). A share waiting on sign-in (`awaiting-sign-in`) tints the icon `text-warning` and shows "Sign-in required (<cli>)" with `SignInAction` (`components/sign-in-action.tsx` over `use-share-sign-in.ts`, polling like `use-cli-login.ts` but never sharing its wire) in the panel and as a strip under the card; the daemon retries after the sign-in and announces `shares_changed`, which re-reads the list (`daemon-client.ts` `onSharesChanged`). Settings → Sharing checks a place only on Check, never on open. Settings → Sharing (`settings/sharing.tsx`) edits the places, the agents switch and the auto-revoke window, and lists every shared page with the moment it was first shared, Copy link, Revoke and Revoke all.

**Shells** (`chats/shell-activity.ts`):

- Detached state comes from the daemon's `shell_open`/`shell_info` rows, never from a text match. `readLaunchHandle` stays strict and anchored at the start of the reply.
- A failed kill is not a kill.
- Foreground commands are gated on `isSettledRunStatus`. Detached commands are not.
- The whole gate lives in `Chats.tsx`'s `shellsByAgent`, and every count flattens that map.
- `shell_info` and `workflow_info` rows are skipped outright in `groupTranscript`.
